\chapter{Runtime safety and foreign trust boundaries}
\label{ch:88}
\index{Runtime safety and foreign trust boundaries}

Ordinary On1x checking controls source types and the resource uses admitted by the model. Canonical verification checks the representation consumed by code generation. Native execution depends on the runtime and on any bridge, archive, or external provider included in the build. A raw extern can have a precise scalar type while still having undocumented side effects or a mismatched implementation. Link and review evidence are needed for those questions.

\section{Name the guarantee you need}
For a JSON view, source and canonical checks address lexical lifetime. For a C pointer returned by a provider, ownership and invalidation need an explicit foreign contract and a compiler representation that enforces it. For a native extension, the loading arrangement determines authority. Check which layer rejects an invalid use before expecting another profile to enforce the same invariant. \cref{ch:95} covers semantic contract transport and \cref{ch:112} covers isolated versus trusted SDK invocation.

\section{Follow a conditional transfer in the C provider}
In the repository's C contract example, allocate an array root and a
separate JSON number containing 42. Initially there are two independent
owners. Ask the provider to append the child. If the append fails,
ownership has not transferred: the caller still destroys the child and
the root. If it succeeds, the root now owns that child. The example
clears the caller's child pointer so that its cleanup cannot destroy
the transferred object a second time. The C pointer's static type did
not change when the call succeeded. Any wrapper modeling the transfer
must therefore know the meaning of the return value.

On the successful path take the first element with
\OnxCodeTerm{mulib\_json\_at}. This is a view into the root, so reading
its number is valid while the root still supplies the object. Serialize
the root and then destroy it. Which of the two returned pointers may
still be used? The child view has expired. A successful serialization
produced an independent string, which the caller can print and free.
Their common history as return values from the same library tells us
nothing about their distinct lifetimes.

This exercise explains why a declaration inventory alone cannot supply
a safe On1x adapter. It must distinguish fresh ownership, a conditional
transfer, and a borrowed result tied to a particular owner. The native
example tests those provider semantics. The checked client facade has
its own smaller set of operations and lifetime rules; compiling this C
example does not add mutation or general pointer values to that facade.

